<section xml:id="Section-1">
<title>Reduced Row Echelon Form (RREF)</title>

       <title>Matrix Representations</title>

      <p>
        Another crucial operation for vectors is the dot product
        <me>\vec{x}\cdot \vec{y} = \sum_{i=1}^{n} x_{i}y_{i}.</me>
        For example, <m>(1,0,-1) \cdot (1,0,1) = 0</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Given a system of linear equations:
        <md>
          <mrow>x + y \amp= 7,</mrow>
          <mrow>y + z \amp= 9,</mrow>
          <mrow>x+z \amp= 11</mrow>
        </md>
        We can rewrite these equations using dot products with vectors,
        <md>
          <mrow>(1,1,0) \cdot (x,y,z) \amp= 7</mrow>
          <mrow>(0,1,1) \cdot (x,y,z) \amp= 9</mrow>
          <mrow>(1,0,1) \cdot(x,y,z)\amp= 11</mrow>
        </md>
        However, to save money and time on parentheses and rewriting <m>(x,y,z)</m>, we just write this using the coefficient matrix as
        <me>\begin{pmatrix} 1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp1 \\ 1 \amp 0  \amp1 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} x\\ y\\ z \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 7 \\ 9\\ 11 \end{pmatrix}.</me>
        If we're being really succinct, I call the coeffient matrix <m>A</m>, the variables <m>\vec{x} =(x,y,z)</m>, and the righthand side <m>\vec{b} = (7,9,11)</m>. Then we finally arrive at the standard way of writing down a system of linear equations
        <me>A \vec{x} = \vec{b}. </me>
        In particular, given an <m>m \times n</m> matrix <m>A</m>, I can define its multiplication with a vector in <m>\mathbb{R}^{n}</m> by
        <me>A \vec{x} = \begin{pmatrix} \vec{a}_{1} \\\vec{a}_{2}  \\ \vdots  \\\vec{a}_{m}  \end{pmatrix} \vec{x} =
        \begin{pmatrix} \vec{a}_{1} \cdot \vec{x} \\\vec{a}_{2} \cdot \vec{x} \\ \vdots  \\\vec{a}_{m} \cdot \vec{x} \end{pmatrix}.</me>
        For example,
        <me>\begin{pmatrix} 0 \amp 1 \amp 0\\ 1 \amp 0  \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \\ 3\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}
         0(1) + 1(2) + 0(3) \\ 1(1) +0(2) + 0(3) \\0 (1) + 0(2) + 1(3)
        \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}
        2 \\ 1\\ 3
        \end{pmatrix}.</me>
      </p>

      <theorem>
        <statement>
          <p>Solving a system of linear equations corresponds to solving the matrix vector equation <m>A \vec{x} = \vec{b}</m>, where <m>A</m> is the coefficient matrix of the linear system and <m>\vec{b}</m> is the right hand side.</p>
        </statement>
      </theorem>

      </section>